/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Two clean steel surfaces pressed together touch only at microscopic high spots.
   - When the film is thick enough to fully separate the surfaces, friction is just the shear of the fluid, and the bearing is called hydrodynamic (Chapter 5.3).
   - Mineral oils are refined from crude petroleum.
   - Synthetic oils are built from chosen chemicals: polyalphaolefins, esters, silicone oils, glycols.
   - Greases are semi-solid: oil held in a soap or clay thickener, plus additives.
   - Solid lubricants are graphite, molybdenum disulphide (MoS₂) and PTFE.
   - τ is the shear stress in the fluid, in Pa; du/dy is the velocity gradient across the film, in 1/s; μ is the dynamic viscosity, in Pa·s, which is the same as N·s/m².
   - ν is the kinematic viscosity, in m²/s; μ is the dynamic viscosity, in Pa·s; ρ is the density of the oil, in kg/m³.
   - Two viscosities exist because they answer two questions.
   - Both values fall steeply as temperature rises.
*/
