/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A sliding contact bearing, also called a plain bearing, carries load on two surfaces that slide over each other.
   - Bearing materials are softer than the shaft, so they wear first and can embed grit instead of scoring the journal.
   - p is the bearing pressure in N/mm²; W is the radial load in N; L is the bearing length in mm; d is the journal diameter in mm.
   - A rolling contact bearing puts hardened steel balls or rollers between an inner ring and an outer ring.
   - The friction torque in either bearing can be written with one coefficient, and the power lost follows from it.
   - T is friction torque in N·m; μ is the coefficient of friction; W is the load in N; r is the shaft radius in m (r = d/2); P is friction power in W; ω is angular speed in rad/s, ω = 2πn/60 with n in rpm.
   - Choose a sliding bearing for heavy shock loads, very large shafts, quiet running or a split housing that must be assembled around the shaft.
   - How well a sliding bearing performs depends on how much oil sits between the surfaces.
   - Rolling bearings also run on a film, but a very thin one squeezed between curved surfaces; it is called an elastohydrodynamic film and it is why the right grease matters even though the metal looks dry.
*/
