/* book exam traps: Using the shaft diameter instead of the radius 0.05 m in T = μ W r, which doubles the torque and the power. | Feeding rpm straight into P = T ω. Convert first: ω = 2πn/60, and only then multiply. | Reading 'antifriction bearing' as 'no friction'. A rolling bearing still has friction, and its starting torque is low but never zero. | Treating bush, babbitt and bronze as the same kind of thing: a bush is a shape, babbitt is a lining material, and bronze can be either a solid bush or a lining. | Assuming a roller bearing is always the better choice. A plain cylindrical roller bearing carries no axial load, so it cannot locate a shaft along its axis on its own. */
